Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

A modern, clean apartment overlooking the gray, rain-swept Puget Sound. 

PETER (30s), ft and sharply dressed, stands by the window holding a mug of coffee. He looks out at the ferries crossing the water.

PETER
Big brother was mine. I’m not talking about some book where the world turns weird. No, I’m talking about my real Big Brother. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Flashback. A gritty, brick ffth-foor walk-up in Milwaukee. 

NICK (20s) is a giant of a man. His face is a puzzle of scars, his reddish-brown hair wild. He carries six heavy shopping bags at once, veins bulging in his massive arms. 

Younger Peter (20s, thinner, wearing a faded pizza parlor shirt) follows behind, carrying a single small bag.

PETER (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. When he walked in a room, people moved out of the way. I lived with him and Ma after dad took off. Nick was my brother. I knew he’d stand up for me no matter what.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter sweeps the foor near the front entrance. 

SIONNA (20s) walks in. She has pale skin, short brown hair, and wears a cheap V-neck shirt with a fsh necklace. She trips on the threshold and bumps directly into Peter's shoulder.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt. She looks up. Her eyes are a piercing, watery light blue. Peter is instantly captivated.

PETER
I work here. I’m just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That’s different. I never heard of that.

SIONNA
My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. The Matron of the River Shannon.

PETER
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She smiles, turns, and walks away. She moves smoothly, like water. Peter watches her go, completely stunned.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

A cramped, worn living room. MA (60s, frail, tired eyes) stares blankly at a row of pill bottles on the kitchen counter. 

Peter gently guides her to a worn armchair facing the TV.

PETER
Your pills are right in front of you in the box for today, Ma.

MA
I can’t fnd my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, what are you talking about? We don’t go to church anymore. 

The front door fies open. Nick stomps in, wearing heavy boots and a suede jacket. He glares at Peter.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don’t know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That’s how old people go downhill. 

PETER
I'm trying, Nick. She's confused.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Holy Christ, do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home. I don’t have the money for that shit.

Nick glares at Peter, his fsts clenching and unclenching. Peter shrinks back slightly.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

A trendy coffee shop. Peter sits across from Sionna. She sips tea. Peter leans in, hanging on her every word.

SIONNA
The environment of the book was like its people. Wild and unpredictable. The love that Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him. A love that never changes will destroy you.

PETER
It reminds me of the way Nick is. Strong and unwavering. But I wonder if it will destroy him in the end too.

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world. You have a purpose, Peter.

PETER
My purpose... what is my purpose?

SIONNA
To fnd the true self within you, and to honor it. 

She reaches across the table and takes his hand.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

PETER
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Loud rock music blares. The bar is packed with working-class locals. 

Nick stands near the pool tables with his buddy BOB (20s, scruffy). Peter stands nearby, holding a beer, looking uncomfortable.

Nick spots his ex-girlfriend, LINDSEY, standing with a DARK-HAIRED MAN. Nick's face hardens. He marches over. Peter and Bob follow.

NICK
What’s up, Lindsey. Who is this?

LINDSEY
Oh, hi Nick. Just an old friend.

NICK
Where did you meet this one? I didn’t hear of you dating anybody.

DARK-HAIRED MAN
What the fuck is wrong with you? You got a problem with me?

Nick's nostrils fare. His fsts clench. The bar goes dead silent. People back away.

PETER
Nick, Nick, listen. You don’t have any problem with this guy. You don’t even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it man, just leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! It’s none of your Goddamn business. You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face, asshole.

PETER
Stop it! You’re crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick's face twists into pure rage. He draws his right fst back and swings at Peter's face. 

Peter jerks backward, dodging the blow. 

The BOUNCER tackles Nick. Chaos erupts. Peter turns and runs out the door.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter sits on Sionna's lumpy couch, shaking. Sionna gently massages his head.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one does that, you know. I mean, no one who really cares about you.

PETER
He does care about me. I mean, he’s my brother. He has always taken care of us.

SIONNA
He is a controlling ass, and you are showing him that you don’t need him. You are the voice of reason, and he doesn’t understand reason. You can’t let him intimidate you, or stop you from your purpose.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Peter sits at a small desk, typing on a bulky computer. Nick paces the living room, smoking a cigarette.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for?

PETER
I'm getting a degree in business. Sionna told me I could qualify for a student loan.

NICK
Whoa, whoa, whoa dude. I can’t believe you are letting this chick tell you what to do. It’s not like she’s gonna be around to help pay this shit back! I can tell you one thing, it sure ain’t gonna be me helping your sorry ass out.

PETER
I don’t do everything Sionna tells me, this was my idea. It would really mean a lot to me if you could just step up and be on my side for once. You are my brother, man.

NICK
Be on your side? How many times have I saved your ass? Just remember this, Bro. Nothin' is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl either. Bro's before ho's. You know what I mean?

Peter stares at Nick, a cold realization settling in his eyes.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY (WEEKS LATER)

Smoke flls the kitchen. Peter rushes in with an oven mitt, grabs a burning coffeepot off the stove, and throws it into the sink. It hisses violently.

PETER
Ma, how many times do I have to tell you NOT to try to cook anything? You’re going to burn the damn building down!

Ma sits in her chair, trembling. She bursts into tears.

MA
I can’t remember anything anymore. I’m no good for anything. Nick doesn’t love me. He never comes to see me anymore.

The front door opens. Nick walks in, surprisingly calm. He looks at Ma, then at Peter.

NICK
Oh, hey. How ya doin' Ma?

MA
Oh my Lord! It’s you Nick! I thought we would never see you again.

NICK
I'll take you to St. Patrick's for four o'clock mass, Ma. Get your coat.

Peter watches, stunned, as Nick gently helps Ma to her feet. 

INT. POLICE STATION - NIGHT

Peter sits across a metal desk from OFFICER DUPUIS (50s, graying mustache). The station buzzes with phones and chatter, but to Peter, it sounds muffled.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Thanks for coming down to the station, Mr. Olson. It seems that there has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother. Last night about eight p.m. they were hit broadside.

Peter stares, his face pale.

OFFICER DUPUIS
I regret to tell you that your brother is in serious condition... and your mother passed away at the scene.

Peter grips the edge of the desk.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology is pending. We think your brother may have been drunk.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Machines beep rhythmically. Nick lies in a hospital bed, battered. A plastic oxygen tube runs into his nose. His right leg is elevated in a massive cast.

Peter stands at the foot of the bed, his face a mask of cold fury.

NICK
Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up, man. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears well up in Nick's bloodshot eyes.

PETER
They took care of it. The mass was nice. She's at peace now.

NICK
I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her to church. The cops are after me now. They're telling me it could be vehicular manslaughter. Like it was all my fault.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
Well, wait a minute. What do you mean? You think I meant to kill her? You think I meant to kill Ma? You son of a bitch!

Nick strains against his tubes, his face twisting in rage. 

Peter just stares at him. He realizes Nick is trapped. Powerless. Peter turns around and walks out without a word.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Sionna stands in her kitchen, wiping her hands on a dish rag. The house is quiet. 

A heavy KNOCK at the door. She walks over and opens it a crack. Nick pushes the door open and steps inside. He looks deranged, his face bruised, eyes wild.

SIONNA
Nick? Is that you? 

NICK
Yeah, it's me. I don't suppose you have any idea what the fuck I've been going through lately, do you?

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally. What makes you think I'm responsible for you going to jail?

NICK
If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive right now. 

Nick steps closer, backing Sionna against the hallway wall.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down. This really has nothing to do with me.

Nick lunges, grabbing her neck with his left hand. He pins her against the wall. Sionna gasps, clawing at his grip.

Nick reaches into his coat pocket and pulls out a switchblade. He ficks it open. The blade gleams.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight or else. Then you're going to get your hands on some cash. I'll be back in one week and I better see some fucking cash or this blade goes into your little neck.

The front door fies open. Peter bursts in.

PETER
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Peter grabs Nick's arm. Nick snarls, driving his elbow back into Peter's head. 

In the struggle, Nick drives the knife hard into Sionna's chest near her collarbone. 

Sionna gasps, a horrible squeaking sound. Blood blooms across her shirt. She slides down the wall.

NICK
Shit. I got to get out of here.

Nick wipes the blade on her shirt, shoves Peter aside, and bolts out the front door.

Peter drops to his knees, ripping his shirt off to press it against Sionna's bleeding chest. 

PETER
Oh God. Stay with me. Stay with me!

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY (MONTHS LATER)

Sionna sits in a rocking chair. Her left arm is in a sling. She stares blankly at the foor, her eyes hollow. 

Peter sits on the couch across from her, desperate.

PETER
Sionna? What's going on?

SIONNA
I don't know. I can't feel myself anymore. 

PETER
You know I love you, right? You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
You say that, but deep inside I still have my doubts. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we just need a break.

PETER
Sionna, don't do that. Stay here with me and we can work things out. Please?

Sionna slowly stands up and walks toward the bedroom, leaving Peter alone in the silence. He buries his face in his hands.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

Return to the present. Peter stands by the window. He turns and picks up a small travel duffel bag from the sofa.

PETER (ON-FRAME)
Seven years of change. Seven years of loneliness. And seven years of hatred for my brother. I had to see Nick. I had to put this behind me forever.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

A sterile, noisy room. Peter sits at a counter, separated by thick plexiglass. 

A door opens on the other side. Nick is led in. He looks incredibly old, scrawny, his hair thinning and gray. He wears an orange jumpsuit. He sits down and picks up the yellow phone receiver. 

Peter picks up his receiver.

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
It's not a cakewalk. But it's looking pretty good for getting out early. Maybe two more years.

PETER
I'm here to tell you some things. And I would appreciate it if you would just listen to me.

Nick looks down at the table, resigned.

NICK
Yeah. Okay, man.

PETER
I'm here to tell you that even though you are my brother... I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me. You're an asshole that killed my mother. You're an asshole that held me down my entire life. But mostly, you're an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life. I'll never see you again because of you, Nick.

Nick stares at the counter, perfectly still.

PETER
But I have to add this bit. I forgive you. I forgive you for being a huge asshole because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Nick looks up. Tears well in his eyes and spill down his weathered cheeks, dropping onto the hard gray counter.

NICK
I'm so sorry. I'm so sorry. You're so right, man. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. It's all I think about. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

PETER
I want you to get better. I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. 

NICK
I love you too. You are a great person. Say, what are you doing now?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We'll be expanding. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

Nick smiles through his tears.

NICK
Wow, man. President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

A GUARD taps Nick's shoulder. Time is up. Nick hangs up the phone and is led away. 

Peter sits for a moment, looking at the empty chair. He smiles, hangs up the receiver, and stands.

INT. AIRPORT TERMINAL - DAY

Peter walks through the bustling airport concourse. His stride is light, confdent. 

PETER (V.O.)
The weight of thirty years was gone. I was free to live without fear, guilt, sadness, or judgment. 

He looks up at the departure board, then continues walking forward, disappearing into the crowd.

FADE OUT.
